# Converting `std::string` to `std::vector`

There are several ways to convert a `std::string` to a `std::vector`, depending on what you want to store in the vector. Here are the most common approaches:

## 1. Vector of characters (most common)

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 2. Vector of bytes (unsigned chars)

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<unsigned char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 3. Vector of strings (split by delimiter)

If you want to split the string into tokens:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <sstream>
#include <algorithm>

std::string str = "one,two,three";
std::vector<std::string> vec;
std::stringstream ss(str);
std::string token;

while (std::getline(ss, token, ',')) {
    vec.push_back(token);
}
```

## 4. Using assign() method

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec;
vec.assign(str.begin(), str.end());
```

## 5. Using range constructor (C++11 and later)

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<char> vec{str.begin(), str.end()};
```

Choose the method that best fits your specific use case. The first method (vector of characters) is the most common when you simply want to convert the string to a vector representation.

